{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["agents/**/*.ts", "lib/**/*.ts"],

  // EXCLUDING THE DIRECTORY IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
  //
  // `exclude` filters the `include` glob. It does NOT stop TypeScript following
  // an import INTO an excluded directory: a file in the program that imports
  // dashboard/lib pulls dashboard/lib into the program anyway, and it is then
  // checked under THIS config's rules rather than the dashboard's own.
  //
  // Measured: with assembled-measure in the program, the root typecheck fails on
  // dashboard/lib/people.ts under noUnusedParameters, which the dashboard's
  // tsconfig does not set. "dashboard" has been in this list all along and did
  // nothing.
  //
  // So the file that CROSSES is excluded BY NAME. It is a diagnostic that
  // imports the real assembleSentence out of dashboard/lib deliberately - a copy
  // of that logic would drift, and the half that drifted would be the half
  // measuring what clients read. It runs with tsx from the command line, which
  // does not consult this list, so excluding it costs nothing.
  //
  // THE ASYMMETRY, and it is in CLAUDE.md: dashboard -> agents is sanctioned via
  // externalDir and only for import-free files. agents -> dashboard is never
  // reachable from a build. Anything added below is the second kind and must be
  // command-line only.
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dashboard",
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/assembled-measure.ts",
    // Same crossing and the same reason: it calls the real buildEntry so the
    // depth it reports is the depth that prints, not a count of stored rows.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/depth-ranking.ts",
    // Same crossing again: it calls the real buildParties and noPartiesNote, so
    // what it measures is what a client document prints rather than a count of
    // stored columns.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/party-gate-measure.ts",
    // And again: it calls the real buildParties to count what a document prints.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/name-as-party-measure.ts",
    // And again. Measures contact_name, which unlike presented_by is genuinely
    // mixed on one column and one stream, so it counts rather than concludes.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/contact-name-measure.ts",
    // Same crossing: it calls the real buildEntry to count what prints.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/repeated-label-measure.ts",
    // And again. Brief Q item 1: the sub-measures it reports - a named party, a
    // stated fact, a condition, a contact - are properties of what an entry
    // PRINTS, so it calls the real buildEntry rather than counting stored rows.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/holdings-judgement.ts",
    // And again, for Brief T. The four criteria it measures - a named party, a
    // stated fact about the scheme, a condition, a decision - are properties of
    // what an entry PRINTS, so it calls the real buildEntry rather than counting
    // filing_facts rows, which would overcount by every fact the entry dedupes,
    // caps or refuses for failing the quotation check.
    "agents/scraper/diagnostics/market-standard.ts",
    // AND THE CHECK ITSELF, which is the first non-diagnostic to cross. It calls
    // the real buildEntry because the four criteria it gates on are properties
    // of what an entry PRINTS: counting filing_facts rows would count facts the
    // entry dedupes, caps or refuses, and the gate would then pass on something
    // no client ever sees. Run by npm run verify with tsx, never by a build.
    "agents/scraper/verify-market-standard.ts"
  ]
}
